\description{
A track view of beta values over a gene, region or set of probes, after SeSAMe's
\code{visualizeGene()}, \code{visualizeRegion()} and \code{visualizeProbes()}, for cohorts that mix
Infinium arrays. Samples are rows and CpGs are columns of the heatmap, evenly spaced
and in genomic order. Above it, the transcripts are drawn to genomic scale, with
lines linking each CpG to its position and an ideogram marking the region.
}
\details{
\strong{Mixed arrays.} Arrays are joined on genomic position (see \code{\link[=methylationCohort]{methylationCohort()}}).
Every CpG on any plotted array is a column, so most columns of a cohort mixing HM450
with EPICv2 will be blank for its HM450 samples: only around a third of the CpGs on
these arrays are on all three. Those cells take \code{absent.color}, separately from
\code{na.color} for a probe the array has but which gave no value, such as one masked by
quality control. Nothing is imputed. \code{cpg.scope = "shared"} keeps only the CpGs on
every plotted array, and the coverage strip under the heatmap shows which array has
which CpG. Probes at one position, whether replicates or different IDs, are averaged.

Beta values are not corrected for differences between arrays, which can be large, so
a cluster following \code{platform} may be technical rather than biological. Grouping on
\code{platform} keeps those comparisons within an array.

\strong{Clustering.} Samples are clustered on the CpGs measured in all of the samples being
clustered. Where fewer than two CpGs are, each pair is compared on those it shares, a
pair with none is placed at 1.1 times the greatest distance, and a note on the figure
says so. These orderings rest on the CpGs of this region alone.

\code{attr(fig, "table")} holds two tables: \code{betas}, the values plotted with each CpG's
position and probes, and \code{samples}, the samples in plotted order.

Unlike \code{visualizeRegion()}, the CpG island and Ensembl regulatory tracks are not
drawn; \code{rowData()} columns of the cohort can be drawn as strips with \code{cpg.tracks}.
}
